/* Lecturer's notes: one framing note per lesson (about 60 to 110 words; sources in _template/lec_data/notes/<course>.json, rebuilt by _template/build_notes.py), in the voice of the module's lecturer (see course-lecturers.js).
   Keyed by module key; the array follows the lesson order. Simulated voice written for teaching. It adds no new facts beyond the
   biography lines checked in course-lecturers.js, and the lesson text stays the authority on content. */
window.COURSE_LNOTES = {
  "greenhouse-year-1": [
    "A greenhouse is a device for managing sunlight. The glass lets the light in, and the same light heats the air, the soil and every surface inside. I built houses at Chatsworth before I built a palace, and in each I asked what jobs the parts did. A small house swings in temperature faster than a large one, because it has little air to soak up the swing, so it needs watching more closely, not less. As you read, list the sources of heat and the ways it escapes. Then write one sentence on what that means for how often you will look.",
    "Pick the structure for a stated purpose, and site it before you buy it. The extension sources set out a ladder from the simplest cover to a full greenhouse, so ask how much cover your purpose needs. Orient the house to the light and the wind, and walk the plot at different times of day. Choose a covering by its trade-offs, not by its price. In my houses every part had more than one job, and I would have you ask the same of yours: what does this frame, this vent, this path do besides the obvious?",
    "Air is the first and cheapest tool, and an undersized vent is the commonest cause of trouble. In the houses I described, the vents swung on pivots and opened by rods from one place, so the air could be adjusted. Work out how much opening you have for the size of your house, and ask whether you can reach it. Add circulation where air sits in layers, and watch moisture, since still damp air invites disease. Try opening vents earlier in the day and compare the log.",
    "You cannot manage what you have not measured, and one thermometer in the wrong place will mislead you. Put sensors where the plants are, out of direct sun, and record at set times. Record the minimum and maximum, and humidity if you can. Read the pattern over several days before you act. At the Palace the readings were taken every two hours from many thermometers, and the habit is what matters: the same place, the same times, written down."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-2": [
    "Think of the year as a harvest to be spread, not only a season to be survived. The sources start from a different premise from most gardeners: extend the harvest, not the growing season, and choose what survives the cold. I wrote that no single border is full all summer without contrivances, and the same is true of a house. As you read, list three crops that stand a cold spell and say where in the year each gives you something. The aim is a steady flow, not one arc.",
    "Every calendar in this module hangs on two dates: the average last frost in spring and the first in autumn. Find them for your place from a local source, and use a range, not a single day. Work backwards from the harvest you want, counting the weeks each crop needs. Where the sources disagree, write both and say which you will test. I said what was true in my place, not everywhere, and you should do the same: mark the dates as yours.",
    "A single sowing gives a glut and then a gap. Sow at intervals of one to three weeks, and pair quick crops with slow ones so that one finishes as the next fills the space. In my borders I planted oriental poppies beside gypsophila, so that the poppies finished as the gypsophila covered the ground. Draw the handover for one bed on a time line, with weeks, and check for gaps and for crowding.",
    "Divide the year into four blocks, each with its own job for the house. Winter is for protecting, not forcing; spring for raising transplants; summer for heat-loving crops and keeping the rest cool; autumn for sowing what will stand the winter. Swap the blocks for your hemisphere. Give each block one purpose and a short list of crops. I gave each border its season, and it kept each one strong rather than half-dressed all year."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-3": [
    "A seed needs the right temperature, moisture, humidity and light, and the medium may differ from the air by several degrees. I chose the pea because it met my conditions and tested it for two years before I used it. Do the same with your seed: check it against the conditions you can give. Make the environment ready before you sow, and write down what you provided. If a seed fails, the cause is more likely a missing condition than bad luck.",
    "Clean containers and a good mix prevent trouble before it starts. The sources disagree on some choices, so set the options side by side and choose one for a stated reason. Fill the container evenly, level the surface and moisten the mix before you sow. Sanitise used trays. I protected my crosses from stray pollen, and a clean tray is the same kind of protection: it keeps out what you did not intend to introduce.",
    "Sowing takes a minute; the first two weeks decide the result. Sow at the right depth and spacing, water without moving the seed, give warmth and cover as needed, and give light as soon as seedlings appear. Check the trays daily and count what you see. I counted every seed and reported the failures, and I would have you keep a log with the number sown, the number up and the number surviving. Watch for damping-off and write down the date you first see it.",
    "A seedling in a tiny cell outgrows it before the bed is ready. Pot on at the right time, handle by the leaf and not the stem, thin the weak ones, and harden off gradually. Count again at each stage so you know the loss. Small trays fluctuate widely, as my plants did when I looked at them one at a time, so judge a seed lot by repeated trays. Record what you did and what you saw."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-4": [
    "Poor growth often has a cause you can name. pH and EC tell you about the medium, and the alkalinity of your water tells you how fast the pH will drift. Learn what each number measures and why it matters. I looked for the missing constituent and then tested the fix. Make a list of the factors you can check before you blame anything else, and put the pH and EC in it. A reading is a clue, not a verdict.",
    "Extract a sample of the medium solution with distilled water and measure the extract, rather than pushing a probe into the pot. The sources describe three methods: the PourThru, the saturated media extract and the 1:2 dilution. Read the EC result against the range for the method you used, because the ranges differ. Do one test the same way each time. When a result looks odd, repeat it before you act, and rule out the other causes before you fix the one you first suspected.",
    "A single test tells you little. The value is in repeating it the same way and watching the trend. Keep your meter calibrated, test on a schedule, and record the date, the bench and the result in one place. Turn the column of numbers into a decision: what changed, what you will adjust, and what you will check next. When one thing is fixed, another may become the limit, so look for the next short stave.",
    "How and when you water decides how much pH and EC move. Collect the guidance on supply, volume and timing into a routine you can log. Check the water you start with, since it can carry alkalinity that shifts the medium. Measure how much a crop uses over a week. Change one thing at a time on one bench and keep a control bench. Write down the result so that the next season starts from knowledge."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-5": [
    "The temperature in a greenhouse is a balance of gains and losses. Sunlight brings energy in, heaters add more, and heat leaves through the covering, through gaps and to the night sky. Plants take a share by transpiring. Draw the balance for your house and mark the biggest gain and the biggest loss. I gave my glasshouse parts more than one job so that each helped manage the heat, and you should ask the same of yours.",
    "Summer is when a greenhouse is most likely to harm its own crop. Use the ladder: choose cooler crops, then natural ventilation, then forced ventilation, then evaporative cooling, and shade last because it also cuts the light plants need. Ventilate first, and shade the outside of the glass, not the inside. At the Palace the vents worked from one position, the blades kept the rain out, and screens hung outside the glass. Write your own hot-day routine in four lines.",
    "Winter asks how little heat you can use and still keep the crop healthy. Begin with crop choice, then add cover, then heat the air safely, then heat the roots if you must. Mix the air gently so that cold does not sit in the corners, and watch moisture. Write the rules for safe heating, and keep fuel and flame away from anything that burns. A plan that works at the coldest night is a plan you have tested before it arrives.",
    "Manual ventilation depends on someone being at the door at the right moment, and in a small house that moment comes quickly. Decide what to automate, and set the trigger from your own log, not from a guess. A set point is a number you have earned by recording. I tested the houses I built and described what they did, and you should test a trigger the same way: set it, log it, and move it if the plants say so."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-6": [
    "Integrated pest management uses cultural, biological, physical, mechanical and chemical tactics together, with scouting and records at the centre. Learn the six components and the four families of control. I asked, before reaching for the spray gun, whether any other method would do. Decide what damage you can tolerate before you start, and write it down. A pest on one leaf is information, and a pest on every plant is a decision.",
    "The cheapest pest control is the pest that never arrives. Before a crop goes in, look at the whole house for places pests can hide, and clean them. Prevent problems through culture, with good spacing and clean water. Keep the house clean and the climate in the range the crop likes, not the pest. Write a short sanitation checklist and run it between crops. Prevention that you have logged is evidence for your review.",
    "Scouting is the habit that makes everything else work. Use sticky cards, walk the same route, and check indicator plants. Record what you find, where and when. Early detection lets you use the gentlest response. Look also for the natural enemies, because I wanted to protect the controls built into the environment. Count the same plants at the same time each week, so the numbers can be compared.",
    "Natural enemies can be bought and released, either a few early or many when the pest is established. Follow the guidelines for a biological programme, and wait for them to work before you judge. Use chemicals only if nothing else will do, sparingly and selectively, and in a way that does not destroy the biological control. Write a decision rule with numbers: at what count do you act, and with what. Review the result."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-7": [
    "Every source you have read ends the same way: keep records. They answer three questions: what did I plan, what happened, and why. I kept my notes month by month, and the notes taught me more than my memory could. Look at what you have been recording already and find the gaps. Write for yourself a year from now, who will not remember what today felt like.",
    "A good log is boring. It has the same columns every time, one row per event, a date on every row and the units written down. Use the five layouts in the lesson, and learn the few spreadsheet functions you need. Mark what is true only in your house, with the words 'in my experience in this place'. Decide how often each log is filled in, and put the schedule on the wall.",
    "The end of a crop is the start of the next one. Record the harvest as it happens, clean out between crops, and reset the structure and the equipment at the turn of the season. Keep the notes short and exact. I wrote what I did and what I saw, and a line for next year. Do the same after each clean-out, so the next crop begins from a known state.",
    "The annual review turns a year of records into a better plan. Follow the five steps: compare the plan with what happened, calculate the results, find the causes, decide the changes, and write them down. Mark which results are only true of your house. Compare with earlier years, because some things show only in the second or third year. Check your review against the five criteria, and be honest about what the data cannot say."
  ],
  "greenhouse-year-cap": [
    "A good capstone is small enough to finish and large enough to teach you something. Start from the fixed limits: your frost dates and the times you will be away. Choose a standard unit, a bed or a block of weeks, and repeat it. I drew a great building from repeated parts to a fixed deadline, after testing the parts on smaller houses. Test one unit first and then commit the year. Write down what you will actually run.",
    "The capstone is marked against five criteria, and each needs evidence collected often enough to leave no gaps. Set up the logs and the checkpoints now. Decide how you will handle missing data honestly: say what is missing and why, and do not fill it in. A log with a gap you have explained is worth more than a tidy log you have invented. Put the checkpoints in your calendar.",
    "At the end of the year you have logs, a calendar and a plan to compare with them. Assemble the deliverable, and check it against the five criteria before you mark it complete. Be plain about what worked and what did not. I would have you ask of each result whether it is a fact about your house or about all houses. Hand in what you can support."
  ],
  "camping-basics-1": [
    "I walked light and let the land decide the route, but I never forgot that someone looks after every acre I crossed. Before you pack anything, find out whose ground you mean to sleep on and what the rules are in that place, because they differ across these islands. Pick one site and write down who owns it, who gives permission and what the written rule says. If you cannot answer the question, you are not ready to go there yet. This is the dull part of a trip that makes the rest possible.",
    "The principles are not a list to recite. They are seven questions to put to your own trip. I argued that people who walk through wild places come to care for them, and care shows itself in what you leave behind. Take a real trip you might make and answer each principle in one line: where you will sleep, what you will do with fire, with waste, with water. Where an answer is thin, plan again. The best sign that you have understood is that the plan changes.",
    "Four jobs cause most of the damage: fire, toilet, washing and rubbish. I used dead roots for a fire and fallen needles for a bed, and I could do that because I took little and asked little of the place. You will usually need a good deal more, so take care with each job. Write down what you will do with each of the four, and what you will carry out. Treat fire with the most respect, because it spreads in ways you cannot reverse, and ask whether you need one at all.",
    "Pick a first site that a beginner can be comfortable on, not one that proves anything. A serviced site with a toilet, water and a warden removes the hard questions while you learn the easy ones. Choose it by looking at what it offers and what it does not, and by asking what you would do if the weather turned. Write one sentence on why this site suits this trip. Go simply, take little, and let the first night teach you what the second should be."
  ],
  "camping-basics-2": [
    "Size and season decide whether the tent serves you or you serve it. The rating roughly equals the number of sleeping pads that fit on the floor, so for comfort take a size larger than the number sleeping in it. Season ratings tell you how the tent copes with wind and cold, and shape tells you how it will behave in a gale. Pick a tent for a named group and a named trip, and say what you will do about space for bags and boots. A tent that suits the trip is a better choice than one that suits the shop.",
    "Pick the pitch before you take the tent out of its bag. Look for ground that drains and is clear of flood, decide where the wind will come from and where the sun will rise, and never pitch under a dead branch or a shallow-rooted tree. Walk the site and say your reasons aloud. If you cannot defend a choice, move the tent. The ground does more for you, or against you, than any piece of kit you carry.",
    "Practise at home. The worst time to learn is after a long day, in the dark, in the rain. Read the instructions, count the parts, and pack a copy. Pitch in order: footprint, poles, body, fly, then peg out and tension so the fly does not touch the inner. Add guylines when the wind is up. Time yourself pitching it twice in the garden and note what slowed you the first time. A routine you have rehearsed is the one that holds when you are tired.",
    "Condensation is warm air meeting a cold surface, and a tent is full of warm breath. Handle it by site, by keeping moisture out and by moving air through. Pitch away from damp hollows, open the vents, keep wet kit out of the sleeping area and cook outside the inner. Make a short list of the three things you will do at bedtime. You cannot stop the water, but you can stop it from deciding the night."
  ],
  "camping-basics-3": [
    "I tested my sleeping gear before I trusted it, and so should you. A bag's rating is an estimate made in a laboratory, not a promise, and your own comfort will not match it exactly. Learn the difference between a comfort figure and a lower limit, and pick the bag by the colder of the two nights you might meet. Write down the figures on your bag and what they mean for your trip. A rating is a starting point, and a test in the garden tells you more than the label.",
    "The ground pulls heat out of you faster than the air does. A pad is as important as the bag, and an air mattress that looks plush may have little insulation. Learn what the R-value means and add pads together when the cold is serious. Build a sleep system on paper: pad, bag, liner, clothes and what goes underneath. Then try it for a night in the garden. If you wake cold, change one thing and try again.",
    "The forecast gives you the air temperature, but the body loses heat to wind, to height and to wet. A bare skin in a breeze feels far colder than the number says. Read the 'feels like' figure as an adjustment, not a separate climate. Plan for the colder conditions you might meet, and know what you will do if you get wet. Write the three ways you lose heat and a control for each.",
    "Go to bed warm and dry, with food in you and dry clothes on. Hypothermia is a drop in body temperature below 35 degrees, and it is a medical emergency. Know the signs: shivering, pale cold skin, slurred speech, slow breathing, tiredness. Rehearse what you will do and whom you will call. Check each other at the end of the day, because a person who is cold often does not notice. Prevention is easier than rescue."
  ],
  "camping-basics-4": [
    "Test the stove in the garden before you rely on it. A camp stove can start a fire, trap an escape route, burn you or give off carbon monoxide. Learn the checks before you light it, and how to read the flame once it is lit. Do the sequence aloud, in order, until it is routine. I chose my cooker by the heat it gave for the weight, and I logged what it did; a trial of your own will tell you more than a label.",
    "Carbon monoxide has no colour, taste or smell, and it can build up in a tent awning. Any stove that burns fuel produces some. Cook outside, keep the stove away from the sleeping area, and never use a stove to heat a closed tent. Write down the signs of poisoning and what you will do: get everyone into fresh air and call for help. This is a hazard you cannot detect by your own senses, so you control it by rules.",
    "Clear water can still make you ill. Germs from human or animal waste can reach lakes, rivers and streams, so water from a clear stream is not safe to drink as it is. Choose a method for the germs you are worried about: boiling, a filter, or treatment. Plan how much you need and how you will carry it. Test the method before the trip, and note the time it takes.",
    "Your fridge is a cool box, so plan the food. Take extra care with cooked meat, eggs and dairy, and keep raw and ready-to-eat foods apart in sealed containers. Make a menu for the trip, with what is eaten first and what keeps. I logged the fuel and time each of my cooking tests needed. Do the same: write the menu and the fuel, and wash your hands before you handle food."
  ],
  "camping-basics-5": [
    "Before the trip I asked those who knew the ground, and I trusted the map only as far as I could read it. Learn the scale and the key, and measure a distance on the map with string or the compass edge. Plan a short route on paper and say what each symbol tells you. A map is a picture of the ground, so check that the picture matches what you see on the day.",
    "A grid reference says exactly where you are. The blue lines form the National Grid: eastings run up the map and northings run across. Give a four-figure reference and then a six-figure one, and say which square of the map each sits in. Practise on places you know. When you are out, an exact reference is what a rescuer needs, so make the habit before you need it.",
    "A phone can fail, and mist hides everything. A baseplate compass does not run out of power. Take a bearing from the map in four steps, walk it with the needle, and allow for the magnetic variation. Practise on open ground first. I turned back on the plan, not on a feeling, and a bearing is a plan you can follow when you cannot see.",
    "Naismith's rule is a simple estimate: one hour for every five kilometres, plus one hour for every six hundred metres of ascent. Use it to set a turn-back time before you set off, and keep to it. Know the weather limits that change the plan, and write them on your trip card. I accepted that the goal was beyond us and turned back with everyone; a limit set in advance makes that choice easier."
  ],
  "camping-basics-6": [
    "Ask three questions before you go: do I have the right gear, do I know the weather, and am I confident in my knowledge and skills? Write the answers on your trip plan. I held that you should check the basic conditions before you guess at a cause, and the same goes for a plan: air, warmth, light, cleanliness, food and who is responsible. If the answer to any question is no, change the plan, not the answer. A plan must also work when you are not there to explain it.",
    "Most harm comes from a short list of hazards. Match each to a control: fire, water, cold, gas, the walk itself. Write the list and the control beside it. Then ask who will do what. A hazard with no named control is still a hazard, and the plan is only as good as the weakest line.",
    "Three illnesses cover the main camp emergencies: cold, gas and dirty water. Each has signs you can see and a response you can give at once. Learn the signs and say them aloud with someone else. Check the basics before you guess at a cause: air, warmth, light, quiet, cleanliness. Observe for a purpose and write down what you see.",
    "When you need help, the order matters. In the hills, dial 999 or 112 and ask for the Police, then Mountain Rescue. Give your prepared details: where you are, how many, what has happened, what you have. Stay where you are until contacted. Practise the call with the details on a card. A calm, complete call is the first step in a rescue."
  ],
  "camping-basics-cap": [
    "The brief is a plan, a kit checklist, a trip card and, after the trip, a one-page review. Plan with margins, roles and a turn-back rule, and give someone else the plan. I turned back at a set point because the sums told us to, and the crew came home. Write the plan so a stranger could act on it. Use your own words and cite what you used.",
    "The capstone passes when each rubric row meets its result. Run the trip, keep a log as you go, and afterwards review honestly: what went well, what you would change, what you would do differently next time. Put it in the ledger so the next trip starts from what you learned. A review that names only successes is a poor one."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-1": [
    "When the grid goes down, the fridge, the tap and the cooker may all fail together, and what is left is a set of habits. The five keys are clean hands and surfaces, separating raw from cooked, cooking through, safe temperatures, and safe water. I found that spoilage comes in on dust, hands and tools, and each key blocks one of those routes. Write one grid-down example for each key in your own kitchen, and mark the key that would fail first in your house. Then ask what you would do about it.",
    "Temperature is the main lever you control without power. Different authorities give slightly different numbers, so set them side by side and note where they differ. I counted spoiled vials by place and by exposure, and I would have you write the numbers down in the same way, in Celsius and Fahrenheit, with the source beside each one. Where the sources disagree, use the stricter number for the person most at risk. A figure you cannot source is a guess.",
    "Cleaning is the first key to fail when the tap runs dry. The hidden route is often the tool or the cloth, as it was with the mercury vat in the experiment I corrected. Plan a way to keep hands and surfaces clean with little water: a measured amount, a sanitiser, a clean cloth for each job. Keep raw and cooked food apart, with separate boards and knives. Write the routine on one card and test it for a day.",
    "Food poisoning is more serious for some people: the very young, older people, pregnant women and those with weakened defences. Make a household risk list with names, not categories. Decide what each person must not eat when the power is out, and who prepares their food. Counting who is at risk is the same habit as counting spoiled vials: it turns a worry into a number you can act on.",
    "If you supply food regularly to the public from home, you are a food business under food law. Registration and control of hazards are rules about the same hidden routes of contamination. Read this lesson as a list of controls you can show to an inspector: what you do, how you check it, and what you record. Where the law is specific, follow the law, and where you are unsure, ask your local authority."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-2": [
    "The first question in a blackout is how long the cold lasts, and the answer is a clock you start when the power goes. Write the time the power went off on the fridge door. A fridge keeps food cold for a limited time if the door stays shut, and a full freezer holds longer than a half-empty one. I observed that warm, rich and exposed food spoils first, and the clock tells you how long you have before it does. Plan how you will stretch it.",
    "Eat in the right order: cooked and opened items first, then chilled foods, then the freezer. The order saves food and cuts risk. Where sources disagree on how long a food lasts, take the shorter time for anyone at risk. I counted the spoiled vials in different places, and the lesson is that a number beats an impression. Rank the contents of your fridge and write the eating order for the first day.",
    "When the power returns, decide by the state of the food, not by hope. Food that is still frozen or has ice crystals may be refrozen under the rules you have learned, and food that has thawed and warmed must be judged by time and temperature. Check three authorities' rules and note the strictest. Heating helps, but it does not last for ever and it does not destroy every spore. When in doubt, the rule is to throw it out.",
    "A short audit after an outage saves you from guessing. Check packaging, dates and temperature in turn, and make a save-or-discard decision for each item. Write the decision and the reason. Keep the audit sheet with your outage log. Repeat the audit the next time, and compare: a record of decisions is the way you improve them.",
    "The freezer is your stock, and the Food Standards Agency's advice sets the numbers for how long it holds. A short outage is unlikely to matter, and a long one will. Treat the freezer as a store with a clock. Write the contents of your freezer, with the date and the portion, so you can decide what to cook first. For a business, label and record, so you can show what you saved and why."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-3": [
    "Water is the fifth key, and it often fails together with the power. I compared two groups of houses that differed only in the water they used, and the lesson is that the source matters more than the look of the water. Learn the three kinds of advisory, and the water you must never use. Check your information from the official source, not from a neighbour. Write down where your water comes from and what you would do if it were declared unsafe.",
    "Boiling is the surest method, and it must be done properly: a full rolling boil for the time your elevation requires. Write the time for your place. Let the water cool without adding anything, and store it covered. I verified each case by evidence, and you can verify the boil by timing it. Practise once so you know how long it takes and how much fuel it uses.",
    "If you cannot boil, you can disinfect small quantities with household bleach, tablets, filters or sunlight, each with limits. Do the sum for the bleach dose by hand, and check the label for the strength. Know what each method does not remove. A rate is only as good as the numbers behind it, and a dose is the same: read the label, measure, and write down what you did.",
    "Safe water has to reach every job in the kitchen, not just the cup. Use it for dishes, for washing produce and for infant formula, and know the rules for babies. List the jobs that use water in your kitchen and name the safe source for each. Track the amount you use in a day, so you know how much to store. A plan that lacks a number is a hope.",
    "A business must provide separate sinks for washing food and for cleaning equipment, and must keep all equipment in good order. When the water fails, some steps must pause. Write which steps depend on water, what you will do with them, and how you will tell customers. Keep a record of the date, the decision and the reason, as I did with the houses and the water. A pause you have written down is easier to defend than one you improvised."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-4": [
    "I fed twelve hundred people on a costed pot, and the lesson was that fuel was the smallest line on the bill. Without power, cooking is a set of options, each with limits: what you can cook on and where. List your options and say where each may be used safely. Foods that need no cooking cost no fuel, so note them. Count what a meal costs you in fuel and time, and see which line is largest.",
    "The commonest deadly mistake in a blackout is cooking indoors on a stove meant for outside. Carbon monoxide has no smell and no colour. Cook outside, keep fuel-burning stoves out of enclosed spaces, and learn the signs. Write the rule on the stove. I kept the heat in with covers and a controlled burn, but I never took a fire into a closed room, and neither should you.",
    "Heat kills most food-poisoning bacteria, but only if it reaches the middle of the food. Use a clean thermometer and check the core. Thaw safely and cook from frozen only where the guidance allows. Reheat to the stated temperature. I closed the damper and covered the boiler to hold the heat, but I would not hold food warm for hours without checking the temperature, and neither should you.",
    "Cooked food left out is a risk even after it has been cooked well. Apply the two-hour rule, and chill or discard on schedule. If someone in the household is ill, do not let them cook for others. Write the rule where everyone can see it. I costed every line of the meal, and a leftover is a line too: if you cannot keep it safe, do not count it as food.",
    "The standard advice is to cook to a core temperature of 70 degrees for 2 minutes, and other pairs of time and temperature are equally safe. Check with a clean probe in the centre. Test your printed reheating instructions for the customer, because the customer's oven is not yours. Record the test, with the date and the result. The costing habit applies to a business too: list every step and what it takes."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-5": [
    "The safest grid-down food never needed a fridge. Build a stock of shelf-stable food, rotate it by date, and know what the dates mean. I put food in sealed bottles and heated them in water, and the lesson is that the method works only if the details are right. Write your stock list with dates and plan a rotation. Eat the oldest first. Check the cans and packets before the emergency, not during it.",
    "A freezer is a store only while it has power, so treat it as a short buffer. Use it to buy time: freeze what you will need first, in portions, and know the ratings. Keep the thermometer where you can see it. Write down what you froze and when. I left room below the rim of every bottle, and a freezer needs room for air to move. Do not overfill, and label everything.",
    "Canning can turn a harvest into a store, but a mistake can be fatal. Low-acid foods need pressure canning, and a jar you do not know the history of must be treated with suspicion. If in doubt, boil it, then discard. I did not know about microbes, and my times are not safe times for your food. Use a tested source for your food and jar size. Follow the full process and check the seal.",
    "Some foods look fine and are not. Learn the signs of spoilage, and the other botulism risks at home, such as oil with garlic or herbs, and baked potatoes wrapped in foil and left warm. Do not taste to test. Write a short list of the signs you will check. A jar that is bulging, leaking or spurting is a jar to discard, whatever the date says.",
    "The Food Standards Agency's rules for freezing in a business are practical: freeze as soon as the food is prepared, divide it into smaller portions, cool cooked dishes before freezing, and label everything. Defrost in the fridge. Label allergens and keep claims to what you can support. I traced failures to method, cork or vessel, and in a business the label is where a failure shows. Write the label as if an inspector will read it."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-6": [
    "Some disasters add contamination on top of the power cut. Floodwater can make food unsafe, and the rule is to throw away what has touched it, with the exceptions the lesson gives for sealed commercial cans. Clean the kitchen surfaces carefully. Write the save-or-discard list. I wrote about prevention first, and the best answer to a flood is to move food up and out of its reach before it arrives.",
    "Under stress you need rules short enough to remember. Write them in short sentences that decide most cases fast: the fridge door stays shut, cooked food goes first, hands are cleaned before food. Know your own risk level, and know the numbers you must remember. Put the page on the fridge. A rule that is on the door is a rule that gets used.",
    "In a long outage your information comes from official voices, not guesses. Know where they speak and what each one covers. If you share food, share it with care: say what is in it, how it was kept and when it was made. Know the water that cannot be made safe, and say so. Roles matter: choose who listens for official news and who writes the log.",
    "A plan beats a panic. Put the pieces together: the clock, the risk list, the eating order, the water plan, the cooking plan. Assign each task to a named person, with a deputy. Set a date for a drill. Run it with the power off for an hour. Hold a short debrief and change one rule. Practise before the emergency, and review after.",
    "A frozen-meal business dispatches on a schedule, and a failure on one day affects every customer on the plan. Write what you will do if the power fails on the day before dispatch, and how you will tell customers. Keep the log. Debrief after any incident and change one rule. I wanted people to drill and to learn from the faults; a business needs both."
  ],
  "grid-down-food-cap": [
    "The brief is a 72-hour grid-down food-safety plan for a realistic household. Name the household, its people and its risks. The plan must contain a clock, a risk list, an eating order, a water plan and a cooking plan. Write the rules short and give each task a name. I marked one habit at a time and checked it daily, and a plan you can score is a plan you can improve.",
    "The model answer sets out the clock with real times: power off at 17:30, the fridge mark at 21:30, the half-full freezer mark at 17:30 the next day. Follow its order: the clock, the risk list, the eating order, the water, the cooking. Compare your plan with it line by line, and note where you differ and why. A model is not an answer to copy, but a shape to check yourself against.",
    "Score your plan against the rubric, then swap with a partner. Mark each criterion with the evidence for it. Give one thing to keep and two to change, and write them down. Run a drill and hold a debrief, as a community of volunteers would. Revise the plan and date the revision. Keep the score so that you can see the improvement.",
    "The capstone for the business is a 72-hour continuity plan for a frozen-product home catering business. Stand it on sources, show the costs, and be honest about the gaps in your evidence. Cost each line, as I did when I fed the house of industry: ingredients, fuel and labour. Say what you do not know. A plan that is honest about its limits is one a customer can trust."
  ],
  "personal-training-1": [
    "A trainer helps one person move more, safely and on purpose, and the first thing to learn is where the job stops. Scope of practice has three zones, and a client scenario can be placed in each. I compared groups to see what activity might do, and I stated only what the comparison could bear. Read this lesson as the same discipline applied to your own work: say what you can do, what you can do only with someone else, and what you must refer. Place three scenarios in the zones before you read on.",
    "The weekly targets are numbers you can quote: 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity, or the vigorous equivalent, or a mix. Learn what counts, and how to combine intensities. When you quote a target to a client, say it is a target for groups, linked to better health, not a promise for one person. I watched what bus workers did and counted their events, and a count is only as good as the way you state it. Convert three mixed weeks into minutes before you move on.",
    "Strength work on two or more days a week, sitting less and starting small are the next three points. Start where the client is and build. The evidence for starting small is that many people stop when they begin too hard. Look at the sedentary behaviour advice and the build rule, and write a first week for a client who does almost nothing. State the limits of the advice: it comes from guidelines for groups, and your client is one person.",
    "Every claim you make to a client rests on some kind of evidence, and the strength of a recommendation is not the same as the certainty of the evidence. Learn what a meta-analysis is, and use the evidence card: what was asked, who was compared, what was measured, what was found, who it speaks for and what it means for this client. Ask who was in the study. A finding about one group may not apply to another, and an observational link is not a cause."
  ],
  "personal-training-2": [
    "For most people the benefits of activity far outweigh the risks, and screening exists to find the few who need a doctor first. Learn which risks are common and which are rare, and who is most at risk. I reported that vigorous exercise appeared protective in groups, and I would have you say the same to a client, with its scope. Practise a one-minute opening: the benefit, why you ask, what you will do with the answers.",
    "The 2015 ACSM algorithm uses three pieces of information: whether the person is active, whether they have a known disease, and whether they have signs or symptoms. Learn the definitions, and then the decision rules. Apply the algorithm to three invented clients and write the advice for each. Do not improvise the decision: follow the steps, and when the steps say refer, refer. Frame the referral as a way of starting safely, not a refusal.",
    "The AAPQ, the PAR-Q and the ACSM algorithm are often discussed, and the evidence on them has limits. Compare what each asks and what each decides. Know what to use in practice, and why. A tool is a way of getting the same answer from every client, and the comparison tells you how reliable that answer is. Write one sentence on what you would use and why.",
    "The first meeting builds trust, screens, learns the client's story and takes baseline measures. Use the outline, which is our design, and adapt it. Take simple measures, and know the red flags during any session: stop and refer. Listen first. Take a baseline you can repeat, so that later numbers mean something. Write the stop-and-refer rule on the file."
  ],
  "personal-training-3": [
    "The best programme fails if the client stops turning up. Self-determination theory names three needs: autonomy, competence and relatedness. Learn the three and give an example of wording that supports autonomy. I wrote my conditions as a hypothesis, not as proven law, and you should hold the evidence in this lesson the same way. Notice what the evidence does not show, and say so.",
    "Motivational interviewing is a counselling method, and the evidence on it has limits, including who delivers it. Learn the core skills the teaching framework gives, and practise reflecting back. Say back what you heard, then check it. I said that the conditions are matters of degree and that the client must perceive them, so the check matters as much as the reflection. Role-play a short conversation and count how many of your turns were reflections.",
    "A goal is a promise about the future, and it must survive real life. Turn an outcome goal into performance and process goals, and add an if-then plan for the day it goes wrong. Use the client's own words. I said what I left out of my claims, and you should say what a goal cannot promise. Write a goal and a plan for the worst week.",
    "Progress tracking is feedback, and feedback supports competence. Decide what to track, how often, and what you will do when things slip. Treat a setback as information. Reflect before you advise. Make sure that the client sees their own progress and that the record is kept in a form they can read."
  ],
  "personal-training-4": [
    "A resistance programme has a few levers: load, volume, frequency, effort and exercise choice. Two principles hold them together, specificity and progressive overload. I looked for what different illnesses share, and in training the shared thing is demand: the body responds to what it is asked to do. Name the levers and write which you would change first for a beginner. Note how much change to expect, and say that the figure is for groups.",
    "Which load is best depends on the outcome. Match a load range to strength, power or jumping, and read the evidence for each. Overall intensity is more than the percentage on the bar. A demand that is too large is a stage of exhaustion, and a demand that is too small leads nowhere. Write the load range for a client goal and the evidence you used, and state the limits of that evidence.",
    "Volume is the total work, and the hard set is the most useful unit. Estimate the effect of adding weekly sets, and say what the data can and cannot tell you. A different cut of the data may change the picture, so look at both. Count the weekly sets in a plan you have written. More is not always better: watch for the signs that the client is not recovering.",
    "Proximity to failure is how close a set ends to the point where no more repetitions are possible. Reps in reserve is a way to describe it without a maximal test. Use it with beginners with care, and check the client's understanding. The evidence is limited, so treat the scale as a guide. Record the reps in reserve for each set, and compare with how the client felt."
  ],
  "personal-training-5": [
    "A session is a set of choices about which movements, in what order, for how many sets. Choose exercises by movement pattern, and put the most demanding first. Rest enough to do each set well. I raised resistance in sets to a 10-repetition maximum for injured servicemen, and later cut the volume I first advised, so I would have you hold your template as a draft. Write one session and count the sets.",
    "This template is an illustrative starting point for a healthy beginner who has passed screening. Count the weekly sets for each muscle group, and check that they fall within the range. Adapt it to the client in front of you. A plan on paper is a hypothesis, and the first weeks test it. Record what the client did, not what you planned.",
    "Progression means raising the demand a little at a time. Use double progression: add repetitions within a range, then add load. When progress stops, look at sleep, food and stress before you change the programme, and consider a lighter week. If there is pain, stop and refer. Write the rule for raising the load and the rule for holding it.",
    "Strength sessions do not replace aerobic activity. Plan the two together, and use a simple talk test to set the effort. Count the weekly minutes against the targets. Say what you will do when the weekly total is short. Keep the plan simple enough to follow on a bad week."
  ],
  "personal-training-6": [
    "Clients ask about protein more than almost anything else. Quote the evidence with its range: a daily target in grams per kilogram, with the uncertainty. Calculate a target for an imagined client, and show the range. Treat nutrition as one more demand on the body, along with training, sleep and work. State the limits of the evidence, and where it comes from groups of people, not the one in front of you.",
    "Protein supplements helped trained people more than novices for fat-free mass, and some details mattered little. Know which subgroup results are strong and which are weak. Write what you would tell a novice and what you would tell a trained client. If the result is weak, say so. A strong claim about a weak result is a mistake you can avoid.",
    "A trainer is not a dietitian. Know which nutrition questions are within scope and which you must refer. Practise the sentence: that is a question for a dietitian or your doctor. Good practice is to give general, evidence-based information and no more. I counted every demand on the body, and a diet plan for a medical condition is a demand you are not trained to size.",
    "The WHO gives specific advice for older adults, for pregnant and postpartum women, and for people with chronic conditions and disability. Adapt the programme, work with the client's clinician, and stay inside your scope. Count the demands in the week: training, work, sleep, illness. Lighten the load when the total is high, and refer when a condition is beyond you. This is not medical advice."
  ],
  "personal-training-cap": [
    "The brief is to take a realistic case-study client and produce a complete coaching file for the first eight weeks. The file holds the screening record and decision, the goals, the plan with numbers and the weekly activity. I ranked the sources of confidence, and I would have you build the plan so that the early weeks give the client mastery. Plan for success first: make sure that the client can complete what you set.",
    "The model outline shows the shape of a good file, not the only correct answer. Compare yours with it: screening, goals, eight-week plan, weekly activity, scope note. Check that each week leans on one source of confidence and that the strongest, personal mastery, comes first. Note where your plan differs and why. Put a number in every section.",
    "Review your file against the rubric, then role-play the first consultation with a partner. Mark each criterion with evidence. Check how often a client completed what was planned, since early failure weighs more. Write what to keep and what to fix, and run the consultation again. Encouragement without something the client has done is the weakest source, so tie your feedback to an achievement."
  ],
  "public-speaking-1": [
    "Public speaking is the designing and delivering of a message to an audience, and I defined rhetoric as observing the available means of persuasion in each case. Start with the conversation: a speaker and a listener who must decide something. Name the three purposes of a speech and the three parts of getting a message heard, and write one speech you might give with its purpose. The art begins with a question of who decides and about what, so ask it of your own speech.",
    "A speaker's character carries weight with an audience, and in my account it is among the means of persuasion. Think about ethics from the start: your intent, your means and your ends. Ask whether you have been honest about your sources, fair to your audience and careful with your claims. Learn the three plagiarism traps and write how you will avoid each. A speech built on a borrowed idea you have not credited persuades no one for long once they find out.",
    "Nerves are a normal response to being evaluated, and the body treats the audience as a threat. Learn the myths and the different kinds of anxiety, and write which ones you recognise in yourself. I separated the speaker's character, the audience's feeling and the proof in the speech, and nerves belong to the first two. Understanding what happens in your body is the first step to using the energy.",
    "Anxiety tends to peak just before you start and then decline as you speak. Plan for the first minute, when you will feel it most, and for the unexpected: a lost note, a noisy room, a question you cannot answer. Prepare, practise and write down what you will do. Rehearse the opening until it is automatic, and the rest will follow."
  ],
  "public-speaking-2": [
    "Hearing is automatic, and listening is purposeful and takes effort. Understand what makes listening hard, and design the speech to help. I held that a speech is made of a speaker, a subject and a listener, and the listener decides its end. Write three things that will make your audience lose the thread, and one thing you will do about each. Keep the listener in mind from the first line.",
    "Audience analysis is learning all you reasonably can about the people you will speak to. Use three kinds: who they are, what they believe and want, and the situation they are in. Avoid stereotypes, and take care not to offend. Remember that the kind of speech depends on who decides and about what: an assembly decides about the future, a court about the past. Write a short profile of a real audience using all three kinds.",
    "When you survey or interview, keep your questions related to your topic, use a standard set, keep it short, say that it is voluntary, and ask at least ten to twenty people. Beware answers given to please. Use what you learn to change what you say, how you open and which examples you choose. Write what the information changed in your plan.",
    "A general purpose is the broad goal: to inform, to persuade or to entertain. Narrow the topic using four constraints and a funnel, and then write a specific purpose statement that says what the audience will understand or do. Test it by asking whether it can be done in the time. A purpose that is too wide is a speech that never ends."
  ],
  "public-speaking-3": [
    "I held that a speaker should gather widely first and then choose. Plan your research time and your search, and keep a log of what you looked for and what you found. Primary research is reported by the person who did it, and secondary research reports other people's work. Gather more than you will use, then keep what supports your purpose. A log lets you find a source again and show that you have not made it up.",
    "Judge a source by four tests: accuracy, authority, currency and objectivity. Popular sources are good for background, and academic sources go through review. Check who wrote it, when, and who paid. Where a source fails a test, say so. A speaker who knows the weakness of the evidence is believed more than one who hides it.",
    "Support has three jobs: it clarifies, builds credibility and makes the speech vivid. Choose among facts and statistics, definitions, examples, narratives, testimony and analogy, and use more than one kind. Keep general arguments ready, and use them where they fit. Use statistics honestly, with the source and the date. Write which kind of support each of your main points will use.",
    "Present each piece of evidence in three steps: setup, execution and analysis. Tell the audience the source's name, where it appears, the date and the expert's standing. Then give the evidence, and then explain what it shows for your point. Choose whether to quote, paraphrase or summarise, and check that you have enough. Hold your points in your memory by their order, and rehearse the steps aloud."
  ],
  "public-speaking-4": [
    "An introduction is only about 10 to 15 percent of the time, so a five-minute speech needs one of no more than 45 seconds. It has five jobs: gain attention, state the purpose, establish credibility, give reasons to listen and preview. I would have you open to make the audience willing, attentive and ready to learn. Write your introduction with those five jobs and time it.",
    "The body is where the purpose is achieved. Brainstorm every point, then group them into two or three main points. Each point should do one job. Choose an organising pattern that suits the purpose, and test it by asking whether the order helps the listener. State the facts of your case clearly, briefly and credibly. Cut any point that does not serve the purpose.",
    "Readers can re-read, and listeners cannot. Use transitions, internal previews, internal summaries and signposts to keep the audience with you. End so that the audience knows it has ended, with the point you want remembered. Rehearse the movement between the points as carefully as the points themselves. Say it plainly, and say it once more at the end.",
    "An outline tests whether your speech holds together: scope against the thesis, the logic of the parts, the relevance of the support, and the balance. Write the full-sentence outline first, then reduce it to a speaking outline and notecards. Practise from the cards, not from a script. Test yourself without the cards, and mark the point where you lost the thread."
  ],
  "public-speaking-5": [
    "Spoken language came long before written language, and listeners hear words once. Choose the exact short word, and use language that is vivid, familiar and appropriate. Learn the difference between a word's plain meaning and what it suggests. I held that the short word is usually the right one. Rewrite one paragraph of your speech in the words you would say aloud, and read both versions.",
    "Judge your words for the speaker, the audience, the context and the topic. Keep the language clear and economical, inclusive and confident. Cut jargon, filler and hedges that weaken your point. Build a climax from facts that all point one way. Read a page aloud and mark each word you stumbled on or did not mean. Replace those words.",
    "Speaking is less formal than reading and more formal than talking. There are four methods of delivery, and each suits different occasions. Choose by the room, the time and the purpose, and plan for a lectern or a microphone. Practise in a room like the real one. I held that the power to speak is cultivated by practice, so give yourself the practice.",
    "Delivery should be clear, interesting and undistracting, with the directness of conversation. The well-known figures on face, voice and words are often misquoted, so use them with care. Practise well: aloud, standing, with a timer, and with someone listening. Record yourself, and mark one thing to keep and one to change. Do it again, and compare."
  ],
  "public-speaking-6": [
    "Aids will not rescue a poor speech. Used well, they improve a good one by helping understanding, memory, interest and credibility. Choose the aid for the job: a chart for a trend, an object for a shape, a slide for a list. I opened with the question my audience avoided, and an aid should do the same kind of work: bring the point into view. Choose one aid for your speech and say which of the four jobs it does.",
    "The people at the back must be able to see or hear any aid. A graphic should take about a third of the slide, with a little large, simple text. Print handouts for after the speech, and have a backup plan if the equipment fails. Test the room before you speak. Rehearse with the aid, and cut it if it distracts.",
    "A good informative speech is accurate, clear and interesting. It exists to give listeners knowledge so that they can make up their own minds. On a controversial topic, give a fair statement of each side. I argued from what my audience already declared it believed, and an informative speech earns trust by starting there. Check each fact and give the source.",
    "Persuasion is an attempt to change what a person thinks or does, in values, attitudes and beliefs. Choose whether to change a belief or a behaviour. Make a claim, give reasons and evidence, and answer the objection the audience will raise. Choose your tone on purpose: argument when it will work, and irony or fire when argument is not enough. Keep to the audience's own principles, and be honest about what you ask."
  ],
  "public-speaking-cap": [
    "The brief is a five-minute extemporaneous speech to inform or persuade a real audience of at least three people. Choose a topic you know or can research in a week, and use at least three sources judged for accuracy, authority, currency and objectivity. Write a full-sentence outline and five speaking cards. Build the climax from facts that all point one way. Plan the week so that you have time to rehearse twice.",
    "Score the recording against the rubric, 0 to 4 on each criterion. A pass is 3 or more on every criterion, or 2 on one with a written plan to improve. Watch the recording twice, first for the whole and then for the parts. Write what you would change and what you would keep. Choose the exact short word where you used a long one. Practise again before the next speech."
  ],
  "sales-basics-1": [
    "Selling follows a sequence of steps, whatever is sold, and I wrote the sale down as a script so that a new salesman could learn it. The textbook's seven steps are a modern version of the same idea. Write them in your own words and say what happens in each. Then test each step for a trick: would the buyer be comfortable reading your page? The Primer itself warned against making a merchant feel he was being sold, and a process is worth having only if it serves the buyer.",
    "Needs are essentials, wants improve life, and emotion pushes both. Learn to tell a reason that is useful from one that is pleasurable, and ask which is driving this buyer. I gave every salesman a territory and asked for a daily record, because I wanted to know who bought and why. Write down the last three things you bought and sort each reason. Then think how you would sell to each of those buyers, and how you would not.",
    "A consumer buys for a household, and a business buys to produce, resell or run an organisation. The buying group in a business can have several people, each with a different concern. Identify who in a company decides, who uses and who pays. Keep a record of each contact. A salesman who knows the group before he calls spends less time in the wrong room.",
    "The business buying process has stages: recognise the need, define it, set specifications, search, ask for proposals, evaluate, decide, and judge afterwards. Features, advantages and benefits turn a product into a reason. State each benefit in the buyer's terms and check that it is true. I told salesmen to show sincerity and sympathy for the buyer's business, and a benefit stated without those is only a claim.",
    "If you supply food or goods to the public from home, you are a business under the law, and you must meet its rules. Registering and controlling hazards is part of selling honestly. Read this lesson as the rules that stand behind the script. Write what you must do before your first sale and who you must tell. When the rule is specific, follow it; when it is not, ask."
  ],
  "sales-basics-2": [
    "Prospecting comes first, because you cannot sell to people you have not identified. A lead is a name, and a prospect is a lead that fits. The funnel shows how many fall away at each step. I gave each salesman a territory so that nobody wandered, and you should write an ideal customer profile in one paragraph. Count the leads you have today and the prospects among them.",
    "The best sources depend on your ideal customer, so ask where that person looks for information. List ten sources and choose the three that fit. A cold call can be respectful if it is brief, honest and ready to end. I wrote down what to say, but never to force. Write the first thirty seconds of a call you would be glad to receive.",
    "Not every lead is worth a presentation. Qualify: is the person ready, willing and able to buy? Five questions will tell you. It is kind to the buyer as well as to you to stop when the answer is no. I asked for a daily record of calls and results, and you should record why you qualified a lead in or out.",
    "Good records turn prospecting into a system. A customer relationship record keeps each contact, the date and the next step. Keep only what you need, and treat personal details with care. I asked for reports every day, and the habit is what matters, not the machine. Set up a simple sheet, fill it in each evening, and review it on Friday."
  ],
  "sales-basics-3": [
    "The preapproach is finding out about a prospect before you contact them. Researching shows you care and lets you sell adaptively. Fill in a precall worksheet: who they are, what they do, what they might need. Find one thing that really interests you. Genuine interest is the first of the six ways to make people like you, and it cannot be faked for long. Write what you learned and what you still do not know.",
    "Great salespeople solve rather than sell. The same camera is a different solution for a teenager and for a grandmother. Write a general benefit statement and a specific one, and set a SMART objective for the call. Talk in terms of the other person's interest. Test each statement: is it true of this person's situation? If not, cut it.",
    "The approach is your first contact, and you are not selling yet. You are introducing yourself and opening the way. Use the six Cs to plan it, and choose phone, email or a visit by what suits the person. Smile, use their name, and listen. Check where the textbook and the law differ on contact, and follow the law. Practise the first thirty seconds aloud.",
    "Choose an approach that fits the situation and the customer, and mix them. Gatekeepers are doing their job, so be courteous and clear. Rejection is part of the work, and it is information. Don't criticise, condemn or complain, whatever the answer. Log each rejection with the reason you were given, and thank the person."
  ],
  "sales-basics-4": [
    "The presentation is where the qualifying and the planning pay off. Prepare the logistics, rehearse, and plan how to adapt. Think from the customer's standpoint: what will they ask, what will they need to see? I treated the window as a craft, and a presentation deserves the same care. Write the three questions the buyer is most likely to ask, and your answers.",
    "SPIN uses four kinds of question in order: situation, problem, implication and need-payoff. Ask more than you tell, and let the buyer talk. The first contact decides the goodwill, so listen well. Write one question of each kind for your own offer, and role-play them. Check that you are not leading the buyer to an answer you chose in advance.",
    "A sales presentation has five steps: build rapport, make a general benefit statement, a specific one, present, then close and recap. Ask how much time you have and who is in the room. Stage the product so it can be seen and handled. Keep your statements dependable. Time your presentation and cut it to fit.",
    "In business sales, closing often depends on whether the value outweighs the cost. Use a cost-benefit comparison and a return on investment, with the arithmetic shown. Write a proposal that a stranger could understand. Use a table of percentages, as I did in the store. Check every number twice before you send it."
  ],
  "sales-basics-5": [
    "An objection is a question or a hesitation, and usually it masks a request for more information. Treat it as an invitation to go on. Many nos really mean 'tell me more'. Write the five reasons people do not buy and a question for each. Prepare for the common ones before the call. Listening to an objection is part of selling for the buyer, not to the buyer.",
    "Price deserves special care. Price is what the customer pays, and value is what they receive. Check that price is the real objection before you answer it, and repeat it as a question. Don't cut the price quickly, because it skips the value conversation. Learn the six types of objection and a response to each. Do not argue: objections thrive on opposition and die with agreement.",
    "Closing starts at the beginning of the selling process. It is asking for the order, not trickery or pressure. Choose a close that fits the buyer and the offer, and use the buyer's own reasons. A cost close compares total cost over time, and it is honest only if the figures are. A no today is better than a no tomorrow, so ask plainly.",
    "Negotiation is discussing an issue between parties with competing interests, and the aim is that seller, buyer and relationship all gain. Know what you can give and what you cannot. Follow up after the sale, to check that the buyer is satisfied. Keep your word. Record what you agreed, and send it in writing."
  ],
  "sales-basics-6": [
    "Ethics are the moral principles that define right and wrong, and a salesperson meets the customer face to face. My firm followed up a rumour about a supplier and acted on what it found, and it took years and cost trade. Spot the dilemma in a sale and choose the honest response. Write a case from your own experience, what you did and what it cost. Honesty is a habit you build in small cases.",
    "Consumer law protects buyers against practices that mislead, pressure or distort the buying decision. Check the tactics from earlier modules against it. A practice that tricks the buyer is unfair even if it works. Write which of the tactics you would remove from your script and why. Keep your claims to what you can show.",
    "Direct marketing by phone or electronic mail is regulated, and you must know who you may contact. Check the rules for live calls, emails and texts, and for the UK and Ireland separately. When in doubt, don't. Write your rule for who you may contact, and keep the evidence of consent. Ask the person if you may follow up.",
    "Good records support honest, lawful selling. If you can identify a person from a contact detail, it is personal data. Tell people who you are and why you hold their details. Write your personal code in five lines and a test for each. Review it each year. A code you have never tested is a hope."
  ],
  "sales-basics-cap": [
    "The brief is a one-page call plan that covers every step of the selling process, with a five-minute role-play. Choose something you could genuinely sell and a real or realistic prospect. Prepare as I would: genuine interest, a worksheet, a benefit in their terms. Smile, use their name, and plan to listen. Write the plan and read it as the buyer would.",
    "Role-play the call with a partner who raises at least two objections. Record it if you both agree, and say why. Avoid the argument, show respect for opinions, and admit mistakes at once. Score the call against the rubric and write what you would change. Ask your partner whether you were sincere. Keep only the lines you would say to a friend."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-1": [
    "A vintage reseller buys older goods for less than a buyer will pay, prepares them, sells them and ships them, and the money is in the gap after every cost. Margin and markup are not the same thing: margin is profit as a share of the price, and markup is profit as a share of the cost. I counted carriage, breakage and failed firings as costs, and you should list every cost of one item, not only what you paid. Work out the true profit on one item before you read on.",
    "Selling a few of your own things is not running a business, and the line is drawn by indicators the authorities publish. Look at how often you sell, whether you buy to sell, and how you present yourself. Write which indicators apply to you today. A returns promise is part of how you sell, and a trader's promises are held to a standard.",
    "Being a trader changes four things: your duties to buyers, your tax position, your platform account, and what the platform reports. Read each as a list of records to keep. I offered guarantees and free delivery as part of how I sold, and a trader's promises need a plan behind them. Write the four changes and a task for each."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-2": [
    "Every sourcing channel trades price against effort, risk and volume. Compare charity shops, car boots, auctions and house clearances on those three. I varied one thing at a time and recorded the result, so test two channels with five items each and compare sell-through and profit. Where the law gives you fewer rights as a buyer, as at auction, buy at your own risk and price the risk in.",
    "The only price that matters is what you can sell for after costs, less the profit you need. Estimate the sale price from sold items, take off the fees, postage and packaging, and set a walk-away rule. Do the arithmetic before you bid. The cheaper carriage rate can cost more once breakage is counted, so count breakage too.",
    "Stock that does not sell is money you cannot spend. Set a budget and a category, check an item before you buy it, and log what you buy and how fast it sells. I tried a small number of things and wrote down the results, and the log is your trial. Repeat the test before you change the plan, because small samples mislead."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-3": [
    "To say an item is vintage you need a reason a buyer could check. Build a date range from evidence of different strengths: marks and documents are strong, style is medium, a story is weak. I named styles and designs in my book, and a resemblance proves nothing about the maker. Write your evidence for one item and give it a strength. Say 'in the style of' unless you have more.",
    "A buyer cannot touch the item, so your words and photographs are the product. Grade the condition with one scale, and disclose every flaw. Clean carefully and say what you did. I specified dimensions and showed the details at large, and you should measure and photograph close up. Write a description with every flaw and every measurement.",
    "Brands and makers add value, so they also attract copies and wishful thinking. Describe what the item says, not what you hope it is. Run a short authenticity routine, and when you cannot answer, say 'I don't know' and leave the claim out. I counted the samples that failed and said how sure my test was; do the same, and word the listing to match what you found."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-4": [
    "Older stock is where reselling meets the criminal law most often. Ivory and precious metals have strict rules, and an item that might be ivory should not be listed until it is confirmed. Hallmarks and weights matter for gold, silver, platinum and palladium. I tested a claim before I made it, and so should you: write the test, the result and the wording. When you cannot test, do not claim.",
    "Older goods were made to older safety standards, and some cannot lawfully be sold by a business today. Check upholstered furniture for its fire labels, and electrical goods for a safe plug and cable. Check the other categories with strict rules. A competent person must test an electrical item before you say it works. Say 'untested, for display or parts' when you cannot test.",
    "A trader selling to a consumer has duties on description, quality, delivery information and cancellation. The goods must match your description. Distance sales carry cancellation rights. Write a returns policy that fits the law. I showed my results with their figures and said how sure I was, and a listing that says what you know and do not know protects both you and the buyer."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-5": [
    "Each platform sends you a different buyer and takes a different cut. Compare them using their own published fee pages, with the date you read them. Think from the customer's side: where would they look for this item? I treated the window as a craft, and the platform is your window. Write a table of three platforms and what each would cost you for one item.",
    "A listing is a promise, and it sells the item and sets expectations at the same time. Photograph from every side, close up on flaws, with something for scale. Write a title and description in a fixed order, and remove every claim you cannot show. Exaggeration is dangerous, and a statement the buyer can check is worth more than a large one.",
    "Price to the market and the ceiling, then measure what you keep. Work out the real profit per item and per hour, including fees, postage, packaging and time. Keep a table of percentages: views, sales, returns. Check the numbers twice. Plan a markdown for items that do not sell, and decide the date in advance."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-6": [
    "Vintage goods are often fragile, awkward or heavy, so shipping decides many of your margins. Pack for the journey and price the postage from the real parcel. Weigh and measure each one. I took the inventory in one day and posted every entry twice, and a packing record works the same way: write the weight, the size and the cost. Keep what the carrier gave you as proof.",
    "Some buyers will ask for returns. A calm, evidence-based process keeps the cost low. Keep your photographs and your description, and offer a fair remedy. Reduce returns by describing accurately in the first place. Record each dispute, the evidence and the outcome. If the totals do not agree, find the mistake.",
    "Keeping records is how you pay the right amount of tax and prove it. Know when tax and filing begin, and compare the trading allowance with your actual expenses by working the numbers both ways. Know what platforms report about you. Close your stock count each week, and keep a ledger whose two sides agree. When they disagree, there is an error to find."
  ],
  "vintage-reselling-cap": [
    "The capstone is a launch pack for your first five items. It joins all six modules in one document. Take the inventory of the five in one day, record each purchase with the date and the terms, and work a price ceiling and profit for each. Check each item against the restricted-goods and safety gates. Write the pack so that a stranger could follow it.",
    "Review the pack as a buyer, a Trading Standards officer and a tax inspector would, one after the other. Run the checks: do the totals agree, do the claims match the evidence, do the records support the figures? Fix a mismatch at its source. Write a review and a next step. A pack whose numbers agree with each other is one you can defend."
  ],
  "cinematography-1": [
    "I wanted exposures short enough to stop a horse in motion, so I learned to count light in the same units on every camera. A stop is a doubling or halving, and the f-number scale and the shutter speeds are both counted in it. Learn to move between the scales without a calculator, because a change of one stop in either place is the same amount of light. Write the scale on a card and count two examples of your own. Once you can count stops you can compare any two settings, and every exposure decision after this is that comparison.",
    "A film camera exposes while a rotating disc is open and moves the next frame in while it is closed, so the angle of the opening sets the exposure time for a given frame rate. The 180 degree shutter is the usual starting point, and it gives the motion blur that audiences take as normal. Work out the shutter speed for 24 and for 25 frames a second at 180 degrees, and then change the angle and see what the blur does. I put a lot of effort into making shutters fast and exact, and you can feel why when you try to freeze a movement with the wrong one.",
    "Once you have chosen your frame rate and shutter angle, brightness has to come from somewhere else: aperture, sensitivity or a neutral density filter. Count the stops between the light you have and the light you want, and pick a filter that removes that many. Do the sum on paper before you put the filter on. If you change the aperture to fix brightness, remember that it also changes the depth of field, so decide which look you are protecting.",
    "Slow motion comes from shooting faster than you play back, so 48 frames a second played at 24 gives half speed. Faster rates also shorten each exposure, so you need more light. Watch for flicker from lights that pulse, and for the skew of rolling shutters when the camera or the subject moves quickly. Test your camera by panning past a vertical line and look at the result. I laid out cameras to catch a sequence step by step, and testing your own gear is the same habit: you want to know what it will do before you rely on it."
  ],
  "cinematography-2": [
    "A stills camera that records video brought a new kind of image to many hands, and with it a set of limits you need to know. The first generation of these cameras had small files, short clips and weak sound. Read what the camera could and could not do, and write the three limits that matter for your shoot. I closed the aperture and lit for it, and I did it because I had tested what the lens and the film would give; the habit is to find out the limits before you build a plan on them.",
    "A sensor's size changes the angle of view a lens gives, and the crop factor lets you convert focal lengths between formats. It also changes the depth of field, the noise and the lenses you can use. Work through the numbers in the lesson by hand: take a lens, say its angle on your sensor, and compare it with the same lens on a larger one. Write one sentence on what a bigger sensor changes in your own scene. Then test two lenses on your camera, because some lose quality at certain stops and the label will not tell you.",
    "A picture profile is a set of tone, colour and sharpness choices baked into the file. A standard profile looks good at once but clips shadows and highlights early, while a flat or log profile keeps more but wants more care and more bits. Learn the exposure aids on your camera and what each one tells you about highlights. Shoot the same scene on two profiles and look at the highlights on a screen. Protect the highlights first, since once they are gone you cannot bring them back.",
    "The camera is a good image recorder and a limited sound recorder. Plan sound, support and card space before the day: a microphone you can monitor, a tripod or rig you can trust, and enough card for the takes. Run the pre-shoot check from the lesson from top to bottom, and write what failed. I tested each lens, because a surprise on the day costs more than a test the day before. Fix what the check finds, then check again."
  ],
  "cinematography-3": [
    "A lens's focal length fixes the angle of view, and the angle of view decides how space looks. Wide lenses stretch distance and make movement toward the camera feel quick, while long lenses compress space. Estimate the horizontal angle of a few lenses from the sensor width and write down what each does to a face and to a room. I found that I could get depth from a small aperture and the right light, and a lens choice is part of the same decision. Try the same shot on a wide and a long lens, and see where you stand to keep the subject the same size.",
    "Depth of field is the range that looks acceptably sharp, and it depends on aperture, focal length and distance. A T-stop corrects the f-stop for light lost in the lens, which is why cine lenses are marked in T. Use the formula in the lesson to compare two setups on paper, then shoot both. Deep focus was a choice I made with light, film and glass, and shallow focus is the opposite choice. Know which one the scene needs before you open the aperture.",
    "The aspect ratio is the width divided by the height, and it shapes how a frame is composed. Choose it for the scene, then choose shot sizes with the vocabulary from the lesson: wide, medium, close-up, and what each adds. Frame with a clear subject and leave space where the person is looking. Draw three frames for your scene in the ratio you chose, and ask what each one tells the audience that the others do not.",
    "Primes are sharper and faster than most zooms, and zooms let you change the frame without moving. Adapters let you fit lenses to a body, but they can cost you something. Test any lens before you trust it, chart in hand, at the stops you plan to use. I tested each lens because some lose quality at certain stops, and your own test will tell you which to keep for your key shots. Write the result in your notes."
  ],
  "cinematography-4": [
    "Colour temperature compares a light with a glowing black body and is measured in kelvins. Daylight and tungsten differ a good deal, and the camera must be told which one it is seeing. Set white balance to match the light, or use a filter, and then check it on a white card. Note the temperature of the lights in your scene before you start. A mismatch is easy to fix in the camera and hard to fix later.",
    "Three-point lighting uses a key, a fill and a back light to model a subject and control the shadows, and a fourth light can light the background. Set the key first, then add fill until the shadows are the depth you want, and count the difference in stops. In the unchained-camera years I worked with lighting that the equipment could hold, and for television I lowered the contrast so the picture held up in the chain. Decide the key to fill ratio for your scene and write it down.",
    "A light has a colour, a level, a direction and a quality. Hard light comes from a small source and casts crisp shadows, and soft light wraps around the subject. Ask where the light in the scene appears to come from, and place your lamps to match. Motivated light has a reason in the world of the scene, such as a window or a lamp. Sketch your set with the motivation for each light, and cut any light you cannot explain.",
    "A meter reads the light and suggests settings, and an incident meter reads what falls on the subject while a reflected meter reads what bounces off. Use the light-level table in the lesson to plan, then check with a meter or the camera's own aids. I built light meters for the work I was doing, because I wanted a number I could trust. Measure your scene, set the exposure, shoot a test and look at it. Adjust one thing at a time."
  ],
  "cinematography-5": [
    "The first film cameras were fastened to a tripod head, so the first films were fixed shots. Every move since then needs a support and a reason. Choose a pan, tilt, track or handheld move and say what it adds to the scene. I took the camera off the tripod, and the reason was that the story needed it to move. Pick the support that suits your kit and check that the move can be repeated. A move with no reason is an effect, and the audience will feel it.",
    "The 180 degree rule keeps the camera on one side of the line between two characters, so the first stays frame right of the second. Draw the line on a plan before you place the camera. Crossing it on purpose can unsettle the audience, so do it only when the story wants it. Check your shots against your plan and watch for continuity of props, eyelines and costume. A diagram on paper saves a day of reshoots.",
    "Coverage means enough positions for the editor to build the scene more than one way: a master, singles, over-the-shoulders and inserts. Write a shot list with the size, the lens, the move and the purpose of each shot. I rehearsed a three-camera set up for four days so that nothing was left to chance. A smaller version of that discipline is a shot list you have walked through with your actors.",
    "On a small shoot one person may operate, pull focus and light, but the jobs still exist. Divide them in writing. Plan the day with times for setting up, rehearsing and shooting, and keep a margin. Think about safety: cables, heights, vehicles and people. I made light meters because I wanted measurements, and the planning habit is the same: write numbers down. At the end of the day, check the schedule against what happened."
  ],
  "cinematography-6": [
    "A film gauge is the width of the strip, and each gauge has a frame size, a run time and a typical use. Compare Super 8, 16 mm and 35 mm on those three. Work out the run time from the length of the film and the frame rate. I made film that anyone could load and send back, and the cartridge in the lesson does a lot of the work for you. Choose the gauge that suits the scene and the budget, and say why.",
    "A film stock has a speed and a colour balance, and a filter can correct the mismatch between the stock and the light, at a cost in stops. Reversal film gives a positive image when developed, and negative film gives a negative to be printed or scanned. Work out the exposure change a filter needs from the data sheet. I treated the film itself as the thing that mattered, and you should read the data before you buy it.",
    "Film is exposed now and developed later, so you cannot see the result on set. That makes the test the key habit. Shoot a test roll of your scene, meter it carefully, protect the film from heat and light, and choose a lab. Keep a shot budget so you know how many feet you can spend. Do not skip the test. If the test fails, change one thing and test again.",
    "Film is a different capture medium, not only a look, and it brings costs as well as qualities. List what you gain and what you pay: grain, latitude, cost per foot, delay before you can see. A DSLR can imitate some of the look, but not all of it. Write one sentence on what film gives your scene that digital would not, and one on what it would cost you. Choose with both lists in front of you."
  ],
  "cinematography-7": [
    "I built my own camera when only copies of others' films were available, and the lesson is that you understand a machine when you know how it draws the picture. Analog television builds a frame from lines, and an interlaced signal holds two fields, each with half the lines, captured one after the other. Learn what a field is and why NTSC and PAL differ. Draw the lines of a frame on paper and mark which belong to which field.",
    "VHS, Hi8 and Betacam differ in era, in quality and in use. Each tape generation loses some of the signal when you copy it. Compare the formats in a table and say which you would shoot on and which you would only watch. A pixel count tells you little about an analog format, so judge it by what you see. Shoot a short test on a camcorder if you can, and look at the result on a screen.",
    "Telecine moves film to video, and the frame rates do not match. For NTSC, 2:3 pulldown spreads 24 film frames over 30 video frames, and for PAL the film is sped up slightly. Work through the numbers in the lesson by hand. I joined existing machines into new ones, and a transfer is a place where machines meet. Note what each method does to the motion and the sound.",
    "Most film is now scanned and finished digitally, and a hybrid workflow joins the old and the new. Plan ingest, storage, grade and delivery before you shoot. Name every clip with its source, date and take. Match the sources so the film and the digital footage look as if they belong together. Estimate the storage you will need and keep a backup. A plan for the workflow is as much part of the shoot as the camera."
  ],
  "cinematography-cap": [
    "The brief is a scene of 60 to 90 seconds, one location, one or two people, with a beginning, a turn and an ending. Write the rubric into your plan, and make the plan complete: shot list, line of action, lighting plan, exposure maths and roll plan. I tripped cameras by wire so that each frame could be compared, and your comparison depends on matching frame rate, shutter angle and framing. Decide what you will match before you shoot.",
    "Work in order. Each step uses a module: exposure, lens, light, movement, film. Shoot the same scene on a digital camera and an analog format. Keep a log of the settings and the takes. Test before the real run, and fix what the test shows. If you cannot match something, note it, because the difference is part of the report.",
    "Ingest the footage, name each clip with its source, date and take, and finish both versions. Compare them honestly: what matched, what did not, and why. I showed the original negative to sceptics, and the report is where you show your evidence. Write what you would change next time, with the numbers to back it. A report that states its limits is more useful than one that claims a win."
  ],
  "directing-1": [
    "I came to directing through the stage, as actor, playwright and manager, so I will tell you what I look for first: the order of the work. Before the first rehearsal there is reading, choosing, planning and listening to designers, and each stage has its own question. As you read this lesson, write down the four lines of the director's job in your own words and put a date beside each one for your own scene. A director who cannot say what the work is for each week will find the weeks do the choosing for them. Notice where the screen version differs, because the same habit takes a different form there.",
    "Read the play before you decide anything about it, and read it more than once. On the first read you will get pictures in your head; keep them, but do not trust them yet. The French scene, cut at each entrance or exit, is a practical tool for finding what each character wants in a stretch of action. When you divide your scene, say aloud what changes at each division, and if nothing changes, you have cut in the wrong place. I would also have you time the reading, because a scene has a pace that only shows when it is spoken, and the pace is part of the story shape.",
    "A theme is a short statement you can act on, not a message you push on the audience. Ask the play questions and let the answers settle, and then ask who is in the room. This lesson wants a one-line theme and a sentence about the audience, and I would have you write both before you plan a single move. Check the theme against your scene: does every beat serve it, or only some? Cut the beat that does not, as I cut only what I could justify. If the theme comes out as a slogan, it is too loud, and the audience will disengage.",
    "On screen nothing in the picture is accidental, so the look must come from the idea. Mise-en-scène is the overall look built from setting, character, lighting and composition, and it answers the same question I put to a stage setting: does it help the words or compete with them? Take your theme and write three things that must be in the frame and three that must not. This is the screen version of removing what the audience looks at instead of listening to. Keep the list short and test it against the scene before you commit to a design."
  ],
  "directing-2": [
    "I taught by setting a small problem and talking it through, and this lesson is two such problems. The given circumstances are the world the actor answers in: who, where, when, and the facts of the play. The 'if' is the question that sets the imagination moving, and it works because it admits that it is only a supposition. Write the circumstances for your scene in one breath, and then write one honest 'if' that does not tell anyone what to feel. Watch for the point where the source differs on what is meant by 'the Method', and keep to the exercises as the book gives them.",
    "A play is too big to swallow whole, so we carve it into units and give each an objective. The objective must be a verb, because a noun makes the actor show an idea instead of doing something. Try this on your own scene: name the units, give each a verb phrase, and test it by saying 'I want to...' aloud. Keep the units few; a dozen small ones will scatter the actor's attention. Then use the tests from the book for a right objective, such as whether it is aimed at the partner, and cut any objective that fails one.",
    "The camera is far more intimate than the old stage, and acting that fills the back row looks false at close range. What the actor does with a thought can be seen in the eyes, so ask for less, not more. In this lesson look at what a director asks for on screen and compare it with the stage version of the same objective. Try the same unit twice, once for a room of forty and once for a lens, and note what you took away. The want stays the same; the size of the action changes. Do not ask for a copy of an earlier take, but re-set the circumstances."
  ],
  "directing-3": [
    "No one acts alone. I asked a student whom he was in communion with at that moment, and he said nobody; I told him that we are always in some contact, even with ourselves. Communion is the give and take between two actors, and adaptation is how each adjusts to the other. In your rehearsal, give the actors something to do to each other, not a feeling to show. Watch whether each adjusts to what the partner does. If they stop adapting, they are playing at the audience, and you will see it at once.",
    "Small objectives matter only if they lead somewhere. The through-line of action is the path through the play, and the super-objective is the aim that gathers every unit toward it. Any detail that does not serve it will stand out as wrong. Name your super-objective as a verb, and try three names, as the book does with Woe from Wit, to see which one explains the most. Then name the counteraction, the force that pulls the other way, because a line without resistance goes nowhere. Check every unit against the line you chose.",
    "A rehearsal week is made of small things: a note, a breath, a pause that lets thought arrive. Give notes you can act on, one at a time, in terms of what to do and not what to feel. Plan the week in landmarks, so that each day has a purpose and the actors know what is wanted of them. Keep the room safe and warm, and stop when someone is distressed. When you try the day-backwards exercise, give it ten minutes, and ask for short, physical answers.",
    "A film set has a thousand moving parts, and the actor must keep the performance true from the start of a take to the end while the editor shapes it afterwards. Your job is to hold the shape of the whole so that the actor can attend to the next moment. Rehearse the unit for the camera, and agree what the actor will do before the take. Remember that the line you built backwards from the scene still runs under every shot, even when the scenes are shot out of order. Keep a note of what each actor needs to remember."
  ],
  "directing-4": [
    "Training the stage begins silent, because most storytelling is not words. I put the actor's body first. Learn the parts of the stage and the order in which they draw the eye, and then learn when a posture overrides them. Try this: stand on the stage with nothing to say, and move so that the audience looks where you want. Notice what your body does when you decide to reveal or hide something. The body can find a choice that thought misses, and this lesson is a way to look for it.",
    "The stage picture is made of distance, level and crossing. Three actors in a line make a flat picture, and one stepping forward makes a triangle with a point of focus. Distance tells the audience about the relationship before anyone speaks. Draw your scene as a series of pictures, mark where the power sits, and ask whether each change serves the story. Build a small database of images you like, and note what each does. When a picture works, find out why before you copy it.",
    "A frame is a picture the director chooses, and the audience cannot look outside it. Composition arranges people, objects and setting inside it, to balance or unbalance, reveal or hide. Keep the line of screen direction clear, and decide before you shoot who is on which side. Take your stage blocking and redraw it for a camera: what must be in the frame, and what can be implied? Do not teach risky movement to an actor without a qualified teacher, and keep the capstone free of falls and lifts."
  ],
  "directing-5": [
    "A shot is a decision about what the audience sees and how it feels. A close-up creates intimacy, and an extreme long shot suggests distance. Choose the size, the lens and the movement for a moment, and say why each choice is motivated. If you cannot say why, change it. I would have you cut the same four shots by length first, then by movement, then by tone, as the methods in the lesson describe, and write what each cut made the viewer feel. I wrote about the clash of images, but you will learn it by cutting.",
    "Continuity is the art of not being noticed. The master shot gives the whole scene, coverage gives the details, and the line keeps the viewer oriented in space. Draw the line for your scene before you set the camera, and cross it only on purpose. Parallel editing lets two actions run together, and you can use it to raise tension. When you plan coverage, list the shots you need, and then check that each one will cut together without confusion.",
    "Kuleshov showed that the audience reads meaning from the order of shots, so the same face can look different beside soup, a child in a coffin or a woman. I took that further and called montage a collision: an idea arises from two independent shots meeting. Try it with two shots of your own and ask what idea appears that is in neither. Discontinuity on purpose can break the smooth flow, but only if you know the rule you are breaking. Keep a log of your cuts and what each did.",
    "A stage has no cut, but it has transitions: lighting changes, set moves, an actor crossing the dark. A transition is the way the audience moves from one scene to another, and it can keep the tension or let it drop. Treat it as an edit: decide where each scene should end and what should carry over. Loops can repeat an image like a refrain. Watch for where to stop, because a transition that goes on too long loses the thread you built."
  ],
  "directing-6": [
    "Fix the performance space early, because designs, ground plan and blocking all depend on it. I started from an empty space and a watching audience, and I would have you start there too. Compare proscenium, thrust and round: how the audience sits changes what they see and how close they feel. Draw a ground plan for each, and put one entrance and one exit on each. If you can stage in all three, you can stage anywhere. For a film, ask whether a location or a soundstage serves the story better.",
    "Designers are collaborators and equal voices, not people who decorate your idea. Before each meeting read the play, list what that designer needs, bring pictures, and say the budget aloud. A budget is a story too: every line says what the production values. Apply the stripping test: add back one element at a time, and keep only what you can justify. When you brief a designer, give the one idea the production serves, as the 1970 Dream did with its white box and its doubled roles.",
    "Tech is where the design joins the actors, and you have little time to do what took weeks in rehearsal. Be a steady presence, tell the actors what to expect, and protect their attention. After the opening, review the work honestly and write down what you would keep and what you would change. On screen, the handover is similar: the picture goes to the editor. Keep your notes short and specific, and give credit where it is due. A review that names only problems teaches less than one that also names what to keep."
  ],
  "directing-cap": [
    "Choose a short scene of two or three characters, about three minutes, from a public-domain play or your own writing. You will direct it twice, once for the stage and once for the screen. Write the French scenes, the objectives as verbs, the super-objective, a ground plan and a shot list before you rehearse. Check each objective against the tests: is it aimed at the partner, is it active, is it clear? The plan will change in rehearsal, but you will know why.",
    "Use the rehearsal order in miniature: a read-through, script work on each French scene, blocking, a stumble-through, scene work and a run. Warm up the group first. Keep your notes in terms of what to do, not what to feel. Shoot only what you rehearsed, and record both versions. Do not ask for a copy of an earlier success; re-set the circumstances each time. Keep the work safe, and stop if anyone is distressed.",
    "Show both versions to a small audience and ask what they believed. Use the question 'I do not believe you' as a test, not a verdict. Score each version against the rubric, mark the moments that did not ring true, and group them by unit. Write one fix for each group, and choose one thing to keep. Then rehearse the fixes and record again. A review that leads to a better next run is worth more than a grade."
  ]
};
